[about]
Title=03 - Key Patterns
Intro=The rules every Homer key follows, so a key can be guessed in a program you have never opened: the word gives the letter, Control does, Shift asks, Shift reverses, Alt Shift is a command with no control, the function keys follow Windows, Insert is the reader's -- with a guessing exchange and the keys that explain the keys. This walk assumes walk two.
Setup=Nothing is needed; a Homer program open to try the keys is a bonus.
Homework=Guess three keys in a Homer program you have not used, then check with Control plus F1.

[step]

Say=Every key in a Homer program is named for a word in its command, and the same word gives the same key in every program. Control plus K is Keywords in DbDo and in FileDir; Control plus F is Find in all three; F2 renames or edits in place everywhere. A key never comes from the middle of a word.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Control plus a letter does something. Shift plus a letter, or Alt plus a letter, asks something and changes nothing. Adding Shift to a doing key reverses or widens it: Control plus M marks, Control plus Shift plus M unmarks; Control plus O opens, Control plus Shift plus O opens converting.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Alt plus Shift plus a letter is a command with no control of its own: Alt plus Shift plus P plays a stream, Alt plus Shift plus R records or runs a report, Alt plus Shift plus E exports.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The function keys follow Windows and Office: F1 help, F2 edit or rename, F3 find again, F4 pick from a list or list the windows, F5 run or refresh, F7 spelling, F10 the menus, F11 the newer version, F12 the AI or the files.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Anything with Insert in it belongs to the screen reader; a Homer program never uses the Insert key, so the two can never collide.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=The rules let a key be guessed, in a program you have never opened. I name a command; the reader says the key the rules give it.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Say Status.
Key=
Hear=Shift plus Z

[step]

Say=Keywords.
Key=
Hear=Control plus K

[step]

Say=Unmark, the reverse of Mark.
Key=
Hear=Control plus Shift plus M

[step]

Say=Play Stream, which has no control of its own.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus Shift plus P

[step]

Say=Check for a newer version.
Key=
Hear=F11

[step]

Say=The keys that explain the keys, the same in every program. Control plus F1 is the Key Describer: on, every key says what it does instead of doing it.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=Key Describer On

[step]

Say=Control plus F1 again turns it off. Hotkeys, in the Help menu, lists every key three ways -- by menu, by key, by command -- generated from the program itself, so it cannot fall behind. And the menus say each command's key as you arrow them.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=No Key Describer

[step]

Say=Access letters in dialogs and menus come from the plain Windows rule: the underlined letter, the first letter of a word in the caption, one per dialog; better no letter than one from inside a word. The kit's check counts them per dialog and refuses a duplicate.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=A planned misstep. Press a key the program does not bind, and nothing happens -- no beep, no message -- because an unbound key is not a mistake; the Key Describer or the Alternate Menu tells you what the key you wanted is.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=What this walk taught. I say the idea or the key; the reader says the command or the name.

[step]

Say=Control plus F1.
Key=
Hear=Key Describer

[step]

Say=Every key three ways, from the program itself.
Key=
Hear=Hotkeys
